From 25504e6aa82717a687f9c36fcf0ec26da60e17fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 05:31:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ggml-backend: compute a CPU split after the next GPU split is
 queued behind a host gate

The host-function version (previous commit) ran the CPU split on CUDA's
callback thread: the gap before each graph went, but the split computed
slower there and the callback added latency.

Now the scheduler's thread keeps the CPU split. When a CPU split reads
only copies from the GPU split before it, and the GPU split after it
reads only copies from pinned host memory, the thread queues on the GPU:
the input copies, an event, a host gate (cuStreamWaitValue32 on a mapped
host word), the next split's input copies and its CUDA graph. Then it
waits for the event, computes the CPU split and opens the gate. The card
starts the next graph a few us after the host is done, with no launch in
between.

The thread must not wait for the stream while the gate is closed. A
first kernel launch can load its module and wait for the device: the
warm-up decode of llama-perplexity hung in cudaFuncGetAttributes
(sum_rows) behind the gate. So the CUDA backend gates only a graph that
will launch as its instantiated CUDA graph, unchanged and used in the
last 5 s; host functions are no longer used.

Exact: perplexity identical, test-backend-sched-host same bits.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01C6MCJC4HTKxvuGjKjqDuqQ
